 a nutrient mix under $80$ cubic meters. To achieve target concentration, the total spend must be at least $$124$. Standard water costs $$0.90$ per cubic meter and nutrient solution costs $$3.60$ per cubic meter. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the minimum volume of nutrient solution the manager can include while meeting both constraints?\n\n> A graphing calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186221845 1071 "An orchard manager must keep a nutrient mix under $80$ cubic meters. To achieve target concentration, the total spend must be at least $\\$124$. Standard water costs $\\$0.90$ per cubic meter and nutrient solution costs $\\$3.60$ per cubic meter. Rounded to one decimal place, what is the minimum volume of nutrient solution the manager can include while meeting both constraints?\n\n> A graphing calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186222485 1064 "Where do the predictor and corrector equations of the two-step Adams–Bashforth–Moulton method come from?\n\nThe method consists of two parts: the **Adams-Bashforth** (**AB2**) **predictor**, and **Adams-Moulton** (**AM2**) **corrector**.\nWe'll begin by deriving the AB2 predictor.\n\nConsider an initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y(a)=c\n$$\n\nWriting the differential equation in integral form, we have\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\int_{x}^{x_\\text{new}} f(t, y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\Delta y = \\int_{x}^{x_\\text{new}} f(t, y(t))\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet $\\Delta x = x_\\text{new} - x$ be the step size, and define the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{t - x}{\\Delta x}.\n$$\n\nThen, $t = x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x$ and $\\mathrm{d}t = \\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x,$ so the integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = \\int_0^1 f(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x))\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nNow, define a new function $g$ of $\\theta$ as the integrand:\n\n$$\ng(\\theta) = f(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + \\theta\\cdot\\Delta x))\n$$\n\nThe Adams formulas result from approximating $g(\\theta)$ by a simple function that agrees with its known values, and then integrating that approximation from $0$ to $1.$ We now determine some known values.\n\n- Evaluating $g$ at $\\theta = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0) & =f(x+0\\cdot Δx,y(x+0\\cdot Δx)) \\\\  & =f(x,y) \\\\  & ={y}^{'}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, evaluating $g$ at $\\theta=-1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-1) & =f(x+(-1)\\cdot Δx,y(x+(-1)\\cdot Δx)) \\\\  & =f(x-Δx,y(x-Δx)) \\\\  & =f({x}_{-1},y({x}_{-1})) \\\\  & ={y}_{-1}^{'},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_{-1}$ is the previous $x$-value and $y'_{-1}$ is the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})).$\n\nSince we have two known values, the simplest reasonable approximation to $g(\\theta)$ is a *straight line* passing through these two points: $(0,y')$ and $(-1,y'_{-1}).$\nThis straight line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\theta ) & ={y}^{'}+\\frac{{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}}{0-(-1)}(\\theta -0) \\\\  & ={y}^{'}+({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by integrating this approximation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}p(\\theta )\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{'}+({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\theta )\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={({y}^{'}\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}){\\theta }^{2})|}_{\\theta \\,=\\,0}^{\\theta \\,=\\,1} \\\\  & ={y}^{'}+\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximation of the increment in $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy={\\int }_{0}^{1}g(\\theta )\\,d\\theta \\cdot Δx\\approx {\\int }_{0}^{1}p(\\theta )\\,d\\theta \\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt is this approximation that we define as the AB2 predictor:\n\n$$\n\\Delta y_p = \\dfrac12(3y' - y'_{-1})\\cdot\\Delta x.\n$$\n\nThe Adams formulae result from approximating $g(\\theta)$ by a polynomial interpolant constructed from known function values and integrating that polynomial from $0$ to $1.$\n\nNotice that evaluating $g$ at $\\theta = 0,$ we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(0) &= f(x + 0\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + 0\\cdot\\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x, y) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, evaluating $g$ at $\\theta=-1$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(-1)\n&= f(x + (-1)\\cdot\\Delta x, y(x + (-1)\\cdot\\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x - \\Delta x, y(x - \\Delta x)) \\\\[5pt]\n&= f(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'_{-1},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y'_{-1}$ is the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y(x_{-1})).$\nSo, to derive the AB2 predictor, we can approximate $g(\\theta)$ by a *linear* interpolant using its values at $\\theta=0$ and $\\theta=-1.$\n\nThe Lagrange basis polynomials are\n\\[\n\\ell_0(\\theta) = \\theta + 1,\\qquad\n\\ell_{-1}(\\theta) = -\\theta,\n\\]\n\nso the interpolant is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(\\theta) &= g(0)\\ell_0(\\theta) - g(-1)\\ell_{-1}(\\theta) \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\cdot(\\theta + 1) - y'_{-1}\\cdot\\theta.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by integrating the interpolant, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_0^1 p(\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\n&= \\int_0^1 \\big(y'\\cdot(\\theta + 1) - y'_{-1}\\cdot\\theta\\big) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\int_0^1 (\\theta + 1) \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta - y'_{-1} \\int_0^1 \\theta \\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\left[\\dfrac12\\theta^2 + \\theta\\right]_0^1 - y'_{-1}\\left[\\dfrac12\\theta^2\\right]_0^1 \\\\[5pt]\n&= y'\\left(\\dfrac12 + 1 - 0\\right) - y'_{-1}\\left(\\dfrac12 - 0\\right) \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac32y' - \\dfrac12y'_{-1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the AB2 predictor is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta y \\approx \\Delta y_p &= \\int_0^1 p(\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\cdot \\Delta x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\left(\\dfrac32y' - \\dfrac12y'_{-1}\\right)\\cdot\\Delta x \\\\[