ain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2,000 &= 32,000 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6} \\\\\n\\frac{2,000}{32,000} &= \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{16} &= \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{16} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= \\frac{t}{6} \\\\\nt &= 4 \\cdot 6 \\\\\nt &= 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $24$ years for the value to reach $\\$2{,}000$.\n\nFinally, to find the year, we add this to the initial year $2015{:}$\n\n$$\n2015 + 24 = 2039\n$$\n\nSo, the value will reach $\\$2{,}000$ in the year ${2039}$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186224918 1074 "We're told that a machine was purchased in $2015$ for $\\[math]32{,}000$, and its value halves every $6$ years. We want to find the year when the value will reach $\\[/math]2{,}000$.\n\nThe formula for exponential decay, in terms of a halving period $d$, is\n\n$$\ny = a \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/d}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=32{,}000$ is the initial value in $2015$.\n- The value halves every $6$ years. So, the halving period is $d=6$.\n- $t$ is the time, in years, after $2015$.\n\nTherefore, the expression for the value $y$ after $t$ years is\n\n$$\ny = 32{,}000 \\cdot \\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $t$ when\n\n$$\ny = 2{,}000\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y = 2{,}000$ in the last equation and simplifying, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2,000 &= 32,000 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6} \\\\\n\\frac{2,000}{32,000} &= \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{16} &= \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{16} = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^4 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{t/6}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4 &= \\frac{t}{6} \\\\\nt &= 4 \\cdot 6 \\\\\nt &= 24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $24$ years for the value to reach $\\[math]2{,}000$.\n\nFinally, to find the year, we add this to the initial year $2015{:}$\n\n$$\n2015 + 24 = 2039\n$$\n\nSo, the value will reach $\\[/math]2{,}000$ in the year ${2039}$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186224922 1071 "An employee’s annual salary is $\\$52,000$ when they start a new job. The salary is modeled by an exponential function $F$, where $F(t)$ represents the salary, in dollars, $t$ years after starting. After $7$ years, the salary grows to $\\$68,428.45$.\n\nRounding the base of the exponential function to two decimal places, write the equation that could define $F$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186224922 1071 "An employee’s annual salary is $\\[math]52,000$ when they start a new job. The salary is modeled by an exponential function $F$, where $F(t)$ represents the salary, in dollars, $t$ years after starting. After $7$ years, the salary grows to $\\[/math]68,428.45$.\n\nRounding the base of the exponential function to two decimal places, write the equation that could define $F$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186224922 1074 "We are told that the salary starts at $\\$52,000$ and grows to $\\$68,428.45$ after $7$ years.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\nF(t) = a b^t\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=52,000$ is the initial salary.\n- $t$ is the time, in years, after the job is started.\n- After $t=7$ years, the salary is $F(7) = 68,428.45$.\n\nNow, we proceed to substitute the previous values in the formula for $F(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(7) &= 52, 000 \\cdot b^{7} \\\\\n68, 428.45 &= 52, 000 \\cdot b^{7} \\\\\n52, 000 \\cdot b^{7} &= 68, 428.45 \\\\\nb^{7} &= \\frac{68, 428.45}{52, 000} \\\\\nb^{7} &= 1.31593…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $b$, we take the $7$-th root of both sides. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= \\sqrt[7]{1.31593…} \\\\\n&= 1.04000… \\\\\n&\\approx 1.04\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation that could define $F$ is\n\n$$\nF(t) = 52,000 \\cdot (1.04)^t\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186224922 1074 "We are told that the salary starts at $\\[math]52,000$ and grows to $\\[/math]68,428.45$ after $7$ years.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\nF(t) = a b^t\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=52,000$ is the initial salary.\n- $t$ is the time, in years, after the job is started.\n- After $t=7$ years, the salary is $F(7) = 68,428.45$.\n\nNow, we proceed to substitute the previous values in the formula for $F(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(7) &= 52, 000 \\cdot b^{7} \\\\\n68, 428.45 &= 52, 000 \\cdot b^{7} \\\\\n52, 000 \\cdot b^{7} &= 68, 428.45 \\\\\nb^{7} &= \\frac{68, 428.45}{52, 000} \\\\\nb^{7} &= 1.31593…\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $b$, we take the $7$-th root of both sides. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= \\sqrt[7]{1.31593…} \\\\\n&= 1.04000… \\\\\n&\\approx 1.04\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation that could define $F$ is\n\n$$\nF(t) = 52,000 \\cdot (1.04)^t\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186224960 1074 "We want the predicted value at $x=2$. Since $x$ counts years after purchase and $y$ gives the value, we read the point on the curve above $x=2$ and project it to the $y$-axis.\n\nFrom the graph, the point above $x=2$ lies between the $5{,}000$ and $10{,}000$ marks on the $y$-axis. From the given options, the closest estimate is $\\$7{,}300$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186224960 1074 "We want the predicted value at $x=2$. Since $x$ counts years after purchase and $y$ gives the value, we read the point on the curve above $x=2$ and project it to the $y$-axis.\n\nFrom the graph, the point above $x=2$ lies between the $5{,}000$ and $10{,}000$ marks on the $y$-axis. From the given options, the closest estimate is $\\[math]7{,}300$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186225066 1071 "During a charity livestream, the event had already received $\\$60$ in donations when it went live. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after the livestream began, the total amount of money donated increased by $140\\%$ of the amount from the previous hour.\n\nWrite equation that best represents this model, where $D(t)$ is the estimated total amount of money, in dollars, donated $t$ hours after the livestream began and $t \\leq 5$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186225066 1071 "During a charity livestream, the event had already received $\\[math]60$ in donations when it went live. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after the livestream began, the total amount of money donated increased by $140\\%$ of the amount from the previous hour.\n\nWrite equation that best represents this model, where $D(t)$ is the estimated total amount of money, in dollars, donated $t$ hours after the livestream began and $t \\leq 5$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186225066 1074 "We are told the initial amount of donations was $\\$60$. The total amount donated increases by $140\\%$ of the previous hour’s total each hour.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nD(t) = a(1+r)^t\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=60$ is the initial amount of money received when the livestream began.\n- $r = 1.4$ is the growth rate ($140\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n- $t$ is the time, in hours, after the livestream began.\n\nTherefore, the equation that best represents this model is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(t) &= 60 \\cdot (1 + 1.4)^{t} \\\\\n&= 60 \\cdot (2.4)^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186225066 1074 "We are told the initial amount of donations was $\\[math]60$. The total amount donated increases by $140\\%$ of the previous hour’s total each hour.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nD(t) = a(1+r)^t\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=60$ is the initial amount of money received when the livestream began.\n- $r = 1.4$ is the growth rate ($140\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n- $t$ is the time, in hours, after the livestream began.\n\nTherefore, the equation that best represents this model is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(t) &= 60 \\cdot (1 + 1.4)^{t} \\\\\n&= 60 \\cdot (2.4)^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186225067 1071 "A bank account is opened with a balance of $\\$5,000$. The balance doubles every $6$ years. How much money will be in the account after $18$ years?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186225067 1071 "A bank account is opened with a balance of $\\[math]5,000$. The balance doubles every $6$ years. How much money will be in the account after $18$ years?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186225067 1074 "We're told that a bank account starts with $\\$5,000$, and the balance doubles every $6$ years. We want to find out how much money will be in the account after $18$ years.\n\nThe formula for exponential growth, in terms of a doubling period $d$, is\n\n$$\nN(t) = a \\cdot 2^{t/d}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=5,000$ is the initial balance.\n- The balance doubles every $6$ years. Therefore, the doubling period is $d=6$.\n- $t$ is the time, in years, after the account is opened.\n\nThus, the correct expression for the balance $N(t)$ is\n\n$$\nN(t) = 5,000 \\cdot 2^{t/6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to find the balance after $t=18$ years. Substituting $t=18$ into the equation for $N(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(18) &= 5, 000 \\cdot 2^{18/6} \\\\\n&= 5, 000 \\cdot 2^{3} \\\\\n&= 5, 000 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 40, 000\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be $\\$40,000$ in the account after $18$ years." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592