ffered on all hiking boots. What is the sale price of a pair of boots originally priced at $\\[math]78$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228716 1074 "To find the sale price, we calculate the discount and subtract it from the original price.\n\nFirst, we calculate $14\\%$ of $\\$78{:}$\n\n$$\n0.14 \\times 78 = 10.92\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract this amount from the original price to obtain\n\n$$\n78 - 10.92 = 67.08\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sale price of the boots is $\\$67.08$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228716 1074 "To find the sale price, we calculate the discount and subtract it from the original price.\n\nFirst, we calculate $14\\%$ of $\\[math]78{:}$\n\n$$\n0.14 \\times 78 = 10.92\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract this amount from the original price to obtain\n\n$$\n78 - 10.92 = 67.08\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sale price of the boots is $\\[/math]67.08$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228721 1071 "An artist earned $\\$20$ from a print sale. This amount represented $p\\%$ of the total print price of $\\$25$. What is the value of $p$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228721 1071 "An artist earned $\\[math]20$ from a print sale. This amount represented $p\\%$ of the total print price of $\\[/math]25$. What is the value of $p$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228721 1074 "We are told that an earning of $\\$20$ is $p\\%$ of the print’s $\\$25$ price.\n\nFirst, we express $p\\%$ of $25$ as a fraction and set it equal to $20$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{100} \\cdot 25 = 20\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{p}{100} \\cdot 25 &= 20 \\\\\n\\frac{p}{100} &= \\frac{20}{25} \\\\\n\\frac{p}{100} &= 0.8 \\\\\np &= 0.8 \\cdot 100 \\\\\np &= 80\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $p$ is ${80}$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228721 1074 "We are told that an earning of $\\[math]20$ is $p\\%$ of the print’s $\\[/math]25$ price.\n\nFirst, we express $p\\%$ of $25$ as a fraction and set it equal to $20$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{100} \\cdot 25 = 20\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{p}{100} \\cdot 25 &= 20 \\\\\n\\frac{p}{100} &= \\frac{20}{25} \\\\\n\\frac{p}{100} &= 0.8 \\\\\np &= 0.8 \\cdot 100 \\\\\np &= 80\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $p$ is ${80}$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228722 1071 "A t-shirt costs $\\$18$ before tax. If a $9\\%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total cost after tax?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228722 1071 "A t-shirt costs $\\[math]18$ before tax. If a $9\\%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total cost after tax?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228722 1074 "To find the total cost after applying the tax, we increase the price by $9\\%$, which means multiplying by:\n\n$$\n100\\% + 9\\% = 109\\% = 1.09\n$$\n\nNow, we compute\n\n$$\n18 \\times 1.09 = 19.62\n$$\n\nSo, the total cost after tax is $\\$19.62$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228722 1074 "To find the total cost after applying the tax, we increase the price by $9\\%$, which means multiplying by:\n\n$$\n100\\% + 9\\% = 109\\% = 1.09\n$$\n\nNow, we compute\n\n$$\n18 \\times 1.09 = 19.62\n$$\n\nSo, the total cost after tax is $\\[math]19.62$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228726 1071 "After a $35\\%$ increase, a conference registration fee is $\\$405$. What was the original fee?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228726 1071 "After a $35\\%$ increase, a conference registration fee is $\\[math]405$. What was the original fee?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228726 1074 "A $35\\%$ increase means the final fee is $135\\%$ of the original fee.\n\nWe convert this percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n135\\%=\\frac{135}{100}=1.35\n$$\n\nLet the original fee be $x$. So, after a $35\\%$ increase, the new value is\n\n$$\n1.35x\n$$\n\nWe are told the increased value is $\\$405$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.35x &= 405 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{405}{1.35} \\\\\nx &= 300\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original fee was $\\$300$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228726 1074 "A $35\\%$ increase means the final fee is $135\\%$ of the original fee.\n\nWe convert this percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n135\\%=\\frac{135}{100}=1.35\n$$\n\nLet the original fee be $x$. So, after a $35\\%$ increase, the new value is\n\n$$\n1.35x\n$$\n\nWe are told the increased value is $\\[math]405$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.35x &= 405 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{405}{1.35} \\\\\nx &= 300\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the original fee was $\\[/math]300$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228731 1071 "Lena bought a drawing tablet for $\\$480$. She estimates that the value of the tablet drops by $25\\%$ each month. What is the estimated value of the tablet exactly $2$ months after she bought it?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228731 1071 "Lena bought a drawing tablet for $\\[math]480$. She estimates that the value of the tablet drops by $25\\%$ each month. What is the estimated value of the tablet exactly $2$ months after she bought it?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228731 1074 "We're told that the tablet's value decreases by $25\\%$ each month. This means that its value in each month is\n\n$$\n100\\% - 25\\% = 75\\%\n$$\n\nof the value of the previous month.\n\nWe are also told that at the time of purchase, the tablet was worth $\\$480$. So,\n\n- the tablet's value after the first month is $(0.75)(\\$480) = \\$360$, and\n- the tablet's value after the second month is $(0.75)(\\$360) = \\$270$.\n\nTherefore, the estimated value of the tablet after $2$ months is $\\$270$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228731 1074 "We're told that the tablet's value decreases by $25\\%$ each month. This means that its value in each month is\n\n$$\n100\\% - 25\\% = 75\\%\n$$\n\nof the value of the previous month.\n\nWe are also told that at the time of purchase, the tablet was worth $\\[math]480$. So,\n\n- the tablet's value after the first month is $(0.75)(\\[/math]480) = \\[math]360$, and\n- the tablet's value after the second month is $(0.75)(\\[/math]360) = \\[math]270$.\n\nTherefore, the estimated value of the tablet after $2$ months is $\\[/math]270$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228736 1071 "Five salespeople made a total of $\\$1,000$ in sales. It is known that one of the salespeople made $\\$400$ in sales. Which of the following can be determined from this information?\n\n1. The average sales per person.\n2. The greatest sales made by any one salesperson.\n3. The least sales made by any one salesperson." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228736 1071 "Five salespeople made a total of $\\[math]1,000$ in sales. It is known that one of the salespeople made $\\[/math]400$ in sales. Which of the following can be determined from this information?\n\n1. The average sales per person.\n2. The greatest sales made by any one salesperson.\n3. The least sales made by any one salesperson." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228736 1074 "The average (or mean) is calculated by dividing the sum of the data points by the number of data points.\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Consider statement I. We are given the total sales amount $(\\$1,000)$ and the number of salespeople $(5)$. So, we can compute the average, given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Average} = \\dfrac{1,000}{5} = \\$200.\n$$\nTherefore, the average sales per person *can* be determined.\n- Consider statement II. We know one salesperson made $\\$400$, but we do not know the sales amounts of the others. It is possible that another salesperson made more. Therefore, the greatest sales *cannot* be determined from the given information.\n- Consider statement III. Similarly, we cannot determine the least sales without knowing the sales for each person. Therefore, the least sales *cannot* be determined from the given information.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228736 1074 "The average (or mean) is calculated by dividing the sum of the data points by the number of data points.\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Consider statement I. We are given the total sales amount $(\\[math]1,000)$ and the number of salespeople $(5)$. So, we can compute the average, given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Average} = \\dfrac{1,000}{5} = \\[/math]200.\n$$\nTherefore, the average sales per person *can* be determined.\n- Consider statement II. We know one salesperson made $\\[math]400$, but we do not know the sales amounts of the others. It is possible that another salesperson made more. Therefore, the greatest sales *cannot* be determined from the given information.\n- Consider statement III. Similarly, we cannot determine the least sales without knowing the sales for each person. Therefore, the least sales *cannot* be determined from the given information.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228849 1071 "A dataset of $19$ different grocery basket totals (in dollars) has a mean of $\\$50$ and a median of $\\$50$. A new dataset is created by multiplying each total by $2$. Which of the following measures has the same value in both the original and new datasets?\n\n1. Mean\n2. Median\n3. Range\n4. Standard deviation" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228849 1071 "A dataset of $19$ different grocery basket totals (in dollars) has a mean of $\\[math]50$ and a median of $\\[/math]50$. A new dataset is created by multiplying each total by $2$. Which of the following measures has the same value in both the original and new datasets?\n\n1. Mean\n2. Median\n3. Range\n4. Standard deviation" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228855 1071 "A local survey polled $1,300$ voters about whether they support a new bond measure. The table below summarizes all $1,300$ responses.\n\n|  | Under $40$ | $40$ and Over |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Support | $370$ | $380$ |\n| Do Not Support | $$? | $300$ |\n\nHow many of the voters under $40$ surveyed responded that they do not support the bond measure?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228855 1071 "A local survey polled $1,300$ voters about whether they support a new bond measure. The table below summarizes all $1,300$ responses.\n\n|  | Under $40$ | $40$ and Over |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Support | $370$ | $380$ |\n| Do Not Support | ? | $300$ |\n\nHow many of the voters under $40$ surveyed responded that they do not support the bond measure?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228867 1071 "A bike shop buys helmets at a wholesale cost of $\\$20.00$ each and sells them at a retail price that is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. During clearance, each remaining helmet is sold at a $40\\%$ discount from the retail price. What is the discounted price of a helmet?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228867 1071 "A bike shop buys helmets at a wholesale cost of $\\[math]20.00$ each and sells them at a retail price that is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. During clearance, each remaining helmet is sold at a $40\\%$ discount from the retail price. What is the discounted price of a helmet?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228867 1074 "First, we are told that the retail price is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. Here, the wholesale cost is $\\$20.00$. So, to calculate the retail price, we multiply $20$ by $180\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 180 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  20 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{180}{100}} \\times  20 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.8 \\times  20 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 36\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nHence, the retail price of a helmet is $\\$36.00$.\n\nNow, we're also told that there is a $40\\%$ discount on the retail price. So, to calculate the amount of the discount, we need to find $40\\%$ of $\\$36.00$.\n\nTo do this, we multiply $36$ by $40\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 40 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  36 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{40}{100}} \\times  36 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.40 \\times  36 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 14.4\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the discount is $\\$14.40$. We subtract this amount from the retail price:\n\n$$\n36 - 14.4 = 21.6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the discounted price of a helmet is $\\$21.60$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228867 1074 "First, we are told that the retail price is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. Here, the wholesale cost is $\\[math]20.00$. So, to calculate the retail price, we multiply $20$ by $180\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 180 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  20 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{180}{100}} \\times  20 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.8 \\times  20 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 36\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nHence, the retail price of a helmet is $\\[/math]36.00$.\n\nNow, we're also told that there is a $40\\%$ discount on the retail price. So, to calculate the amount of the discount, we need to find $40\\%$ of $\\[math]36.00$.\n\nTo do this, we multiply $36$ by $40\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 40 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  36 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{40}{100}} \\times  36 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.40 \\times  36 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 14.4\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the discount is $\\[/math]14.40$. We subtract this amount from the retail price:\n\n$$\n36 - 14.4 = 21.6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the discounted price of a helmet is $\\[math]21.60$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228868 1071 "A pair of gloves has a regular price of $\\$35$. During a weekend sale, the gloves are sold at a price that is $40\\%$ less than the regular price. This sale price is also $40\\%$ more than what the retailer paid per pair. What was the retailer’s cost for each pair of gloves?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228868 1071 "A pair of gloves has a regular price of $\\[math]35$. During a weekend sale, the gloves are sold at a price that is $40\\%$ less than the regular price. This sale price is also $40\\%$ more than what the retailer paid per pair. What was the retailer’s cost for each pair of gloves?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228868 1074 "There is a $40\\%$ discount on the regular price. Here, the regular price is $\\$35$. So, to calculate the amount of the discount, we need to find $40\\%$ of $\\$35$.\n\nTo do this, we multiply $35$ by $40\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 40 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  35 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{40}{100}} \\times  35 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.40 \\times  35 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 14\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the discount is $\\$14$. We subtract this amount from the regular price:\n\n$$\n35 - 14 = 21\n$$\n\nHence, the sale price of a pair of gloves is $\\$21$.\n\nNow, we're also told that the sale price is $40\\%$ more than the retailer’s cost per pair. So, the sale price is\n\n$$\n100\\% + 40\\% = 140\\%\n$$\n\nof the retailer’s cost.\n\nWe found that the sale price is $\\$21$. So, if $p$ was the retailer’s cost for each pair, then we have the following equation, which we can solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 140 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  p & \\displaystyle  = 21 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{140}{100}} \\times  p & \\displaystyle  = 21 \\\\ \\displaystyle 1.4 p & \\displaystyle  = 21 \\\\ \\displaystyle p & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{21}{1.4}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 15\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the retailer’s cost per pair of gloves was $\\$15$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228868 1074 "There is a $40\\%$ discount on the regular price. Here, the regular price is $\\[math]35$. So, to calculate the amount of the discount, we need to find $40\\%$ of $\\[/math]35$.\n\nTo do this, we multiply $35$ by $40\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 40 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  35 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{40}{100}} \\times  35 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.40 \\times  35 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 14\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of the discount is $\\[math]14$. We subtract this amount from the regular price:\n\n$$\n35 - 14 = 21\n$$\n\nHence, the sale price of a pair of gloves is $\\[/math]21$.\n\nNow, we're also told that the sale price is $40\\%$ more than the retailer’s cost per pair. So, the sale price is\n\n$$\n100\\% + 40\\% = 140\\%\n$$\n\nof the retailer’s cost.\n\nWe found that the sale price is $\\[math]21$. So, if $p$ was the retailer’s cost for each pair, then we have the following equation, which we can solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 140 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  p & \\displaystyle  = 21 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{140}{100}} \\times  p & \\displaystyle  = 21 \\\\ \\displaystyle 1.4 p & \\displaystyle  = 21 \\\\ \\displaystyle p & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{21}{1.4}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 15\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the retailer’s cost per pair of gloves was $\\[/math]15$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228869 1071 "A craft store sells cutting mats at a retail price that is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. During a seasonal sale, each mat is discounted by $25\\%$ from the retail price. If the final selling price of a mat is $\\$33.75$, what is the wholesale cost per mat?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228869 1071 "A craft store sells cutting mats at a retail price that is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. During a seasonal sale, each mat is discounted by $25\\%$ from the retail price. If the final selling price of a mat is $\\[math]33.75$, what is the wholesale cost per mat?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228869 1074 "Let $p$ be the wholesale cost per mat.\n\nFirst, we are told that the retail price is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. So, to calculate the retail price, we multiply $p$ by $180\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 180 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  p & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{180}{100}} \\times  p \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.8 p\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nHence, the retail price is $1.8p$ dollars.\n\nNow, we're also told that the mat is discounted by $25\\%$ from the retail price. The amount of the discount is:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 25 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  ( 1.8 p ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{25}{100}} \\times  1.8 p \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.45 p\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final selling price is the retail price minus the discount:\n\n$$\n1.8p - 0.45p = 1.35p\n$$\n\nWe are told this equals $\\$33.75$, so we solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.35p &= 33.75 \\\\\np &= \\frac{33.75}{1.35} \\\\\n&= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the wholesale cost per mat is $\\$25.00$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228869 1074 "Let $p$ be the wholesale cost per mat.\n\nFirst, we are told that the retail price is $180\\%$ of the wholesale cost. So, to calculate the retail price, we multiply $p$ by $180\\%{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 180 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  p & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{180}{100}} \\times  p \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.8 p\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nHence, the retail price is $1.8p$ dollars.\n\nNow, we're also told that the mat is discounted by $25\\%$ from the retail price. The amount of the discount is:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 25 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  ( 1.8 p ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{25}{100}} \\times  1.8 p \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.45 p\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final selling price is the retail price minus the discount:\n\n$$\n1.8p - 0.45p = 1.35p\n$$\n\nWe are told this equals $\\[math]33.75$, so we solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.35p &= 33.75 \\\\\np &= \\frac{33.75}{1.35} \\\\\n&= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the wholesale cost per mat is $\\[/math]25.00$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228870 1071 "A luggage store lists a travel backpack with a set regular price. During a holiday promotion, the backpack is sold at a price that is $20\\%$ less than the regular price. The discounted price is $140\\%$ of the final selling price. If the final selling price is $\\$32.00$, what is the regular price?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228870 1071 "A luggage store lists a travel backpack with a set regular price. During a holiday promotion, the backpack is sold at a price that is $20\\%$ less than the regular price. The discounted price is $140\\%$ of the final selling price. If the final selling price is $\\[math]32.00$, what is the regular price?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228870 1074 "Let $p$ be the regular price.\n\nThere is a $20\\%$ discount on the regular price. Subtract this from the regular price:\n\n$$\np - 0.20p = 0.80p\n$$\n\nHence, the discounted price is $0.80p$ dollars.\n\nWe are also told that the discounted price is $140\\%$ of the final selling price. Here, the final selling price is $\\$32.00$. So,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 140 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  32 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{140}{100}} \\times  32 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.4 \\times  32 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 44.8 .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThus, the discounted price is $\\$44.80$. Since the discounted price also equals $0.80p$, we solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.80p &= 44.8 \\\\\np &= \\frac{44.8}{0.80} \\\\\n&= 56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the regular price is $\\$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{56.00}$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228870 1074 "Let $p$ be the regular price.\n\nThere is a $20\\%$ discount on the regular price. Subtract this from the regular price:\n\n$$\np - 0.20p = 0.80p\n$$\n\nHence, the discounted price is $0.80p$ dollars.\n\nWe are also told that the discounted price is $140\\%$ of the final selling price. Here, the final selling price is $\\[math]32.00$. So,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 140 \\mathrm{\\% } \\times  32 & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{140}{100}} \\times  32 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1.4 \\times  32 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 44.8 .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThus, the discounted price is $\\[/math]44.80$. Since the discounted price also equals $0.80p$, we solve for $p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.80p &= 44.8 \\\\\np &= \\frac{44.8}{0.80} \\\\\n&= 56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the regular price is $\\[math]\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid gainsboro]{56.00}$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228871 1071 "Mateo has $310$ British pounds $(\\textrm{£}310)$ that he wants to exchange for US dollars at a currency kiosk. The exchange rate is $\\textrm{£}1 = \\$1.34$. The kiosk charges a $2.5\\%$ fee on the converted US dollar amount. How many US dollars will Mateo receive after the $2.5\\%$ fee?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228871 1071 "Mateo has $310$ British pounds $(\\textrm{£}310)$ that he wants to exchange for US dollars at a currency kiosk. The exchange rate is $\\textrm{£}1 = \\[math]1.34$. The kiosk charges a $2.5\\%$ fee on the converted US dollar amount. How many US dollars will Mateo receive after the $2.5\\%$ fee?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228871 1074 "We are told that $\\textrm{£}1$ is equivalent to $\\$1.34$. So, to convert $\\textrm{£}310$ to US dollars, we multiply it by the exchange rate:\n\n$$\n310 \\cdot 1.34 = 415.4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{£}310$ is equivalent to $\\$415.4$.\n\nNow, because Mateo pays a $2.5\\%$ fee on the converted amount, he receives\n\n$$\n(100 - 2.5)\\% = 97.5\\%\n$$\n\nof the converted US dollars.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n415.4 \\cdot 97.5\\% = 415.4 \\cdot 0.975 = 405.015\n$$\n\nwe conclude that Mateo receives a total of $\\$405.015$ after the conversion." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228871 1074 "We are told that $\\textrm{£}1$ is equivalent to $\\[math]1.34$. So, to convert $\\textrm{£}310$ to US dollars, we multiply it by the exchange rate:\n\n$$\n310 \\cdot 1.34 = 415.4\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{£}310$ is equivalent to $\\[/math]415.4$.\n\nNow, because Mateo pays a $2.5\\%$ fee on the converted amount, he receives\n\n$$\n(100 - 2.5)\\% = 97.5\\%\n$$\n\nof the converted US dollars.\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n415.4 \\cdot 97.5\\% = 415.4 \\cdot 0.975 = 405.015\n$$\n\nwe conclude that Mateo receives a total of $\\[math]405.015$ after the conversion." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228878 1071 "A discount of $14\\%$ is offered on all hiking boots. What is the sale price of a pair of boots originally priced at $\\$78?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228878 1071 "A discount of $14\\%$ is offered on all hiking boots. What is the sale price of a pair of boots originally priced at $\\[math]78?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186228878 1074 "To find an amount that's $14\\%$ *less than* $\\$78,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 14\\% = 86\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n86\\% = \\dfrac{86}{100} = 0.86\n$$\n\nSo, to find $86\\%$ of $\\$78,$ we multiply $\\$78$ by $0.86{:}$\n\n$$\n0.86 \\cdot \\$78 = \\$67.08\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sale price of the hiking boots is $\\$67.08.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186228878 1074 "To find an amount that's $14\\%$ *less than* $\\[math]78,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% 