f this price:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30%\\times 90 & =\\frac{30}{100}\\times 90 \\\\  & =0.3\\times 90 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tax on the jacket is $$27.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186229609 1074 "First, the marked-up price is $150\\%$ of the original cost $($60):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}150\\%\\times 60 & =\\frac{150}{100}\\times 60 \\\\  & =1.5\\times 60 \\\\  & =90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the marked-up price of the jacket is $$90.$\nThen, the tax is $30\\%$ of this price:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}30\\%\\times 90 & =\\frac{30}{100}\\times 90 \\\\  & =0.3\\times 90 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tax on the jacket is $\\$27.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186229610 1071 "What number is $15%$ less than $80?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186229610 1071 "What number is $15\\%$ less than $80?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186229610 1074 "To find a number $15%$ *less than* $80,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-15%=85%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n85%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $85%$ of $80,$ we multiply $80$ by $0.85:$\n\n$$\n0.85\\cdot 80=68\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186229610 1074 "To find a number $15\\%$ *less than* $80,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-15\\%=85\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n85\\%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $85\\%$ of $80,$ we multiply $80$ by $0.85:$\n\n$$\n0.85\\cdot 80=68\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186229613 1071 "A store's sales revenue grew to $110 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of last month's total. Of this revenue, $50%$ came from online orders. What percentage of last month’s total revenue came from online orders?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186229613 1071 "A store's sales revenue grew to $110 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of last month's total. Of this revenue, $50\\%$ came from online orders. What percentage of last month’s total revenue came from online orders?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186229616 1071 "After a $10 \\mathrm{\\% }$ discount, the price of a used bicycle is $$450$. What was the original price?The original price of the bicycle was $$$ {{field-1}}.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186229616 1071 "After a $10 \\mathrm{\\% }$ discount, the price of a used bicycle is $\\$450$. What was the original price?The original price of the bicycle was \\$ {{field-1}}.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186229617 1071 "A store marked down the price of a blender to $85 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of its original cost. The tax on the blender is $12 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of its marked-down price. If the blender originally cost $$200$, what is the tax on the blender?The tax on the blender is $$$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186229617 1071 "A store marked down the price of a blender to $85 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of its original cost. The tax on the blender is $12 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of its marked-down price. If the blender originally cost $\\$200$, what is the tax on the blender?The tax on the blender is \\$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186229620 1071 "After a $25 \\mathrm{\\% }$ discount, the price of a jacket is $$120$. What was the original price?The original price of the jacket was $$$ {{field-1}}.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186229620 1071 "After a $25 \\mathrm{\\% }$ discount, the price of a jacket is $\\$120$. What was the original price?The original price of the jacket was \\$ {{field-1}}.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186230167 1064 "In many real-world situations, such as in retail or finance, it is necessary to apply multiple percentages in succession.\n\nTo do so, we perform one percentage calculation and then apply another percentage to the *result* of the previous calculation.\nThis means each new percentage is based on a *new amount*, not on the original number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $40\\%$ of $150,$ and then $25\\%$ of the result.\n\nFirst, we find $40\\%$ of $150{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40%\\times 150 & =\\frac{40}{100}\\times 150 \\\\  & =0.4\\times 150 \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after the first step, the result is $60.$\nThen, we find $25\\%$ of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25%\\times 60 & =\\frac{25}{100}\\times 60 \\\\  & =0.25\\times 60 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after applying both percentages in succession, the final answer is $15.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186230167 1064 "In many real-world situations, such as in retail or finance, it is necessary to apply multiple percentages in succession.\n\nTo do so, we perform one percentage calculation and then apply another percentage to the *result* of the previous calculation.\nThis means each new percentage is based on a *new amount*, not on the original number.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find $40\\%$ of $150,$ and then $25\\%$ of the result.\n\nFirst, we find $40\\%$ of $150{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40\\%\\times 150 & =\\frac{40}{100}\\times 150 \\\\  & =0.4\\times 150 \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after the first step, the result is $60.$\nThen, we find $25\\%$ of the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25\\%\\times 60 & =\\frac{25}{100}\\times 60 \\\\  & =0.25\\times 60 \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after applying both percentages in succession, the final answer is $15.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186230169 1064 "In real-world applications, it's common to estimate values. However, estimation often leads to **error**, which is the difference between a quantity's estimated and actual values.\n\nFor example, if a real estate agent estimates that a house is worth $\\$180\\,000$ but its actual market value is $\\$150\\,000$, then the total error in the agent's estimate is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{error} = \\$180\\,000 - \\$150\\,000 = \\$30\\,000\n$$\n\nIs this an acceptable amount of error?\n\nErrors by themselves are often difficult to interpret. For example, an error of $1$ centimeter is acceptable when designing a parking lot, but such an error could have serious consequences when performing medical procedures.\n\nTo resolve this, it's often more helpful to compute the **percentage error**. The formula for the percentage error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage error} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{estimate} - \\textrm{exact value} \\mid}{\\textrm{exact value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nNotice that this is virtually identical to the formula for percentage change. The only difference is the terminology used.\n\nLet's go back to our real estate question. The estimated value is $\\$180\\,000$, and the exact value is $\\$150\\,000$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\text{percentage error} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| \\text{estimate} - \\text{exact value} |}{\\text{exact value}}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| 180 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 - 150 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 |}{150 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| 30 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 |}{150 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{30 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}{150 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{15}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.2 \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 20 \\mathrm{\\% } .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the percentage error in the agent's estimation is $20\\%$, which is relatively high." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186230169 1064 "In real-world applications, it's common to estimate values. However, estimation often leads to **error**, which is the difference between a quantity's estimated and actual values.\n\nFor example, if a real estate agent estimates that a house is worth $\\[math]180\\,000$ but its actual market value is $\\[/math]150\\,000$, then the total error in the agent's estimate is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{error} = \\[math]180\\,000 - \\[/math]150\\,000 = \\[math]30\\,000\n$$\n\nIs this an acceptable amount of error?\n\nErrors by themselves are often difficult to interpret. For example, an error of $1$ centimeter is acceptable when designing a parking lot, but such an error could have serious consequences when performing medical procedures.\n\nTo resolve this, it's often more helpful to compute the **percentage error**. The formula for the percentage error is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage error} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{estimate} - \\textrm{exact value} \\mid}{\\textrm{exact value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nNotice that this is virtually identical to the formula for percentage change. The only difference is the terminology used.\n\nLet's go back to our real estate question. The estimated value is $\\[/math]180\\,000$, and the exact value is $\\[math]150\\,000$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\text{percentage error} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| \\text{estimate} - \\text{exact value} |}{\\text{exact value}}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| 180 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 - 150 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 |}{150 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| 30 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 |}{150 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{30 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}{150 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{15}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.2 \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 20 \\mathrm{\\% } .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the percentage error in the agent's estimation is $20\\%$, which is relatively high." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186230170 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to apply a percentage increase to a number to get a new, larger number. In this lesson, we’ll work backwards, finding the *original value* when we know the percentage increase and the new value.\n\nFor example, suppose that after a $35\\%$ increase, a conference registration fee is $\\$405$. What was the original fee?\n\nIncreasing a number by $35\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 35\\% = 135\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n135\\%=\\frac{135}{100}=1.35\n$$\n\nNow, let the original fee be $x$. Then, after a $35\\%$ increase, the new value is\n\n$$\n1.35x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the increased value is $\\$405$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.35x &= 405 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{405}{1.35} \\\\\nx &= 300\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original fee was $\\$300$.\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by applying a $35\\%$ increase to $\\$300{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\$ 300 \\times  ( 100 \\mathrm{\\% } + 35 \\mathrm{\\% } ) & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 300 \\times  135 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 300 \\times  1.35 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 405 \\hspace{1em} {\\color{green}✓}\\end{array}}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186230170 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to apply a percentage increase to a number to get a new, larger number. In this lesson, we’ll work backwards, finding the *original value* when we know the percentage increase and the new value.\n\nFor example, suppose that after a $35\\%$ increase, a conference registration fee is $\\[math]405$. What was the original fee?\n\nIncreasing a number by $35\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 35\\% = 135\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n135\\%=\\frac{135}{100}=1.35\n$$\n\nNow, let the original fee be $x$. Then, after a $35\\%$ increase, the new value is\n\n$$\n1.35x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the increased value is $\\[/math]405$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1.35x &= 405 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{405}{1.35} \\\\\nx &= 300\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original fee was $\\[math]300$.\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by applying a $35\\%$ increase to $\\[/math]300{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\$ 300 \\times  ( 100 \\mathrm{\\% } + 35 \\mathrm{\\% } ) & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 300 \\times  135 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 300 \\times  1.35 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 405 \\hspace{1em} {\\color{green}✓}\\end{array}}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186230173 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to apply a percentage decrease to a number to get a new, smaller number. In this lesson, we’ll work backwards, finding the *original value* when we know the percentage decrease and the new value.\n\nFor example, suppose that after a $5\\%$ decrease, a conference registration fee is $\\$228$. What was the original fee?\n\nDecreasing a number by $5\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 5\\% = 95\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n95\\%=\\frac{95}{100}=0.95\n$$\n\nNow, let the original fee be $x$. Then, after a $5\\%$ decrease, the new value is\n\n$$\n0.95x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the decreased value is $\\$228$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.95x &= 228 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{228}{0.95} \\\\\nx &= 240\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original fee was $\\$240$.\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by applying a $5\\%$ decrease to $\\$240{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\$ 240 \\times  ( 100 \\mathrm{\\% } - 5 \\mathrm{\\% } ) & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 240 \\times  95 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 240 \\times  0.95 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 228 \\hspace{1em} {\\color{green}✓}\\end{array}}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186230173 1064 "In a previous lesson, we saw how to apply a percentage decrease to a number to get a new, smaller number. In this lesson, we’ll work backwards, finding the *original value* when we know the percentage decrease and the new value.\n\nFor example, suppose that after a $5\\%$ decrease, a conference registration fee is $\\[math]228$. What was the original fee?\n\nDecreasing a number by $5\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 5\\% = 95\\%\n$$\n\nConverting this percentage to a decimal, we have\n\n$$\n95\\%=\\frac{95}{100}=0.95\n$$\n\nNow, let the original fee be $x$. Then, after a $5\\%$ decrease, the new value is\n\n$$\n0.95x\n$$\n\nWe are told that the decreased value is $\\[/math]228$. So, we set them equal and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0.95x &= 228 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{228}{0.95} \\\\\nx &= 240\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original fee was $\\[math]240$.\n\nWe can check that this answer is correct by applying a $5\\%$ decrease to $\\[/math]240{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\$ 240 \\times  ( 100 \\mathrm{\\% } - 5 \\mathrm{\\% } ) & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 240 \\times  95 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 240 \\times  0.95 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\$ 228 \\hspace{1em} {\\color{green}✓}\\end{array}}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186230174 1064 "Now, let's consider the reverse problem. Suppose we know that the original price of the meal was $\\$10$ and the new price is $\\$12$. We know that the manager applied a $20\\%$ increase, but how can we determine this percentage change from the initial and final prices alone?\n\nTo compute a percentage change, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage change} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{new value} - \\textrm{original value} \\mid}{\\textrm{original value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- This formula defines percentage change as the ratio of the absolute change in a quantity (the numerator) relative to its original value (the denominator), expressed as a percentage.\n- Note the presence of the absolute value in the numerator. This allows us to apply it to both percentage increases and percentage decreases, as percentage change should always be positive.\n\nNow, in our situation, we have the following information:\n\n- $\\textrm{new value} = \\$12$\n- $\\textrm{original value} = \\$10$\n\nSo, to compute the percentage change, we use the given formula as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\text{percentage change} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| \\text{new value} - \\text{original value} |}{\\text{original value}}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| 12 - 10 |}{10}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| 2 |}{10}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{10}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.2 \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 20 \\mathrm{\\% } .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, the percentage increase is $20\\%$, exactly as we saw earlier." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186230174 1064 "Now, let's consider the reverse problem. Suppose we know that the original price of the meal was $\\[math]10$ and the new price is $\\[/math]12$. We know that the manager applied a $20\\%$ increase, but how can we determine this percentage change from the initial and final prices alone?\n\nTo compute a percentage change, we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{percentage change} = \\dfrac{\\mid \\textrm{new value} - \\textrm{original value} \\mid}{\\textrm{original value}} \\times 100\\%\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- This formula defines percentage change as the ratio of the absolute change in a quantity (the numerator) relative to its original value (the denominator), expressed as a percentage.\n- Note the presence of the absolute value in the numerator. This allows us to apply it to both percentage increases and percentage decreases, as percentage change should always be positive.\n\nNow, in our situation, we have the following information:\n\n- $\\textrm{new value} = \\[math]12$\n- $\\textrm{original value} = \\[/math]10$\n\nSo, to compute the percentage change, we use the given formula as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\text{percentage change} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| \\text{new value} - \\text{original value} |}{\\text{original value}}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| 12 - 10 |}{10}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{| 2 |}{10}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{10}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{5}} \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.2 \\times  100 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 20 \\mathrm{\\% } .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nSo, the percentage increase is $20\\%$, exactly as we saw earlier." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186231384 1074 "To convert $408$ inches into yards, we convert $408$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $408$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{ft} =12 \\, \\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\, \\textrm{ft} =1 \\, \\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $408$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $408{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 408 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 408 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{408}{12}ft|= 408in\n$$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{408}{12}$ as the division $408 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$4$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$4$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$Therefore, $408\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals $34\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $34$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{yd} =3 \\, \\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{yd} =1 \\, \\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $34$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $34{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 34 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 34 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{34}{3}yd|= 34ft; 11\\frac{1}{3}yd|= 34ft\n$$Therefore, $34\\,\\textrm{ft}$ equals $11\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion, $408\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals\n\n$$\n11\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186231384 1074 "To convert $408$ inches into yards, we convert $408$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $408$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{ft} =12 \\, \\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\, \\textrm{ft} =1 \\, \\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $408$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $408{:}$$$\n\\displaystyle 408 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 408 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{408}{12}ft|= 408in\n$$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{408}{12}$ as the division $408 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$4$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{4 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 8}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$4$$8$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$4$$8$$\\phantom{0}$$0$Therefore, $408\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals $34\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $34$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion\n$1\\, \\textrm{yd} =3 \\, \\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{yd} =1 \\, \\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $34$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $34{:}$$$\n\\displaystyle 34 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 34 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{34}{3}yd|= 34ft; 11\\frac{1}{3}yd|= 34ft\n$$Therefore, $34\\,\\textrm{ft}$ equals $11\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion, $408\\,\\textrm{in}$ equals\n\n$$\n11\\,\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186231620 1071 "The total monthly cost (in dollars) that a music streaming service charges for downloading $n$ audiobooks is given by the expression $8 + 2n$. How much does it cost to download $5$ audiobooks in a month?It costs $$$\n{{field-1}}\nto download $5$ audiobooks in a month." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186231620 1071 "The total monthly cost (in dollars) that a music streaming service charges for downloading $n$ audiobooks is given by the expression $8 + 2n$. How much does it cost to download $5$ audiobooks in a month?It costs \\$\n{{field-1}}\nto download $5$ audiobooks in a month." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186231621 1071 "The total cost (in dollars) to rent a bike for $n$ hours is given by the expression\n$\\frac{45}{4} + \\frac{n}{2}$.\nHow much does it cost to rent a bike for $\\frac{3}{2}$ hours?It costs $$$\n{{field-1}}\nto rent a bike for $\\frac{3}{2}$ hours." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186231621 1071 "The total cost (in dollars) to rent a bike for $n$ hours is given by the expression\n$\\frac{45}{4} + \\frac{n}{2}$.\nHow much does it cost to rent a bike for $\\frac{3}{2}$ hours?It costs \\$\n{{field-1}}\nto rent a bike for $\\frac{3}{2}$ hours." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186231713 1071 "A gym charges a one-time registration fee of $$25$ plus a monthly membership fee of $m$ dollars. If the total cost for the first month is $$60$, which equation can be used to find $m$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186231713 1071 "A gym charges a one-time registration fee of $\\$25$ plus a monthly membership fee of $m$ dollars. If the total cost for the first month is $\\$60$, which equation can be used to find $m$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186231728 1071 "A gym charges a one-time registration fee of $$25$ plus a monthly membership fee of $m$ dollars. If the total cost for the first month is $$60$, what is the value of $m$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186231728 1071 "A gym charges a one-time registration fee of $\\$25$ plus a monthly membership fee of $m$ dollars. If the total cost for the first month is $\\$60$, what is the value of $m$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186231766 1071 "A shipping company charges $$3$ per cubic inch for sending a package. John wants to send a package that is $\\frac{5}{4}in$ long, $\\frac{4}{3}in$ wide and $2in$ high. How much does John have to pay for sending the package?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186231766 1071 "A shipping company charges $\\$3$ per cubic inch for sending a package. John wants to send a package that is $\\frac{5}{4}in$ long, $\\frac{4}{3}in$ wide and $2in$ high. How much does John have to pay for sending the package?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186231770 1071 "A bank has created a list of customers with various balances in their bank accounts. The data is shown below.\n\n| Customer | Bank Balance |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Mr. Jones | $- $329$ |\n| Ms. Chin | $$16$ |\n| Ms. Hernandez | $$1256$ |\n| Mr. Pulev | $- $67$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Mr. Jones owes $$329$ to the bank.\n2. Ms. Chin has the lowest bank balance.\n3. Mr. Pulev owes less money to the bank than Ms. Chin." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186231770 1071 "A bank has created a list of customers with various balances in their bank accounts. The data is shown below.\n\n| Customer | Bank Balance |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Mr. Jones | $-\\$329$ |\n| Ms. Chin | $\\$16$ |\n| Ms. Hernandez | $\\$1256$ |\n| Mr. Pulev | $-\\$67$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. Mr. Jones owes $\\$329$ to the bank.\n2. Ms. Chin has the lowest bank balance.\n3. Mr. Pulev owes less money to the bank than Ms. Chin." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186231784 1071 "A bar of chocolate is $18cm$ long, $\\frac{5}{2}cm$ wide, and $\\frac{3}{5}cm$ high. If each cubic centimeter of chocolate costs $$0.04$, what is the value of the chocolate bar?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186231784 1071 "A bar of chocolate is $18cm$ long, $\\frac{5}{2}cm$ wide, and $\\frac{3}{5}cm$ high. If each cubic centimeter of chocolate costs $\\$0.04$, what is the value of the chocolate bar?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186232035 1064 "Many real-world situations involve an unknown quantity that we can determine using an equation. By translating a real-world description into an equation, we can solve for the unknown value.\n\nFor example, a concert ticket costs a base fee of $\\$18$ plus a service charge of $s$ dollars. If the total price of the ticket is $\\$45$, what is the value of $s$?\n\nThe total cost is made up of two parts:\n\n- the base ticket fee of $\\$18$, and\n- the service charge of $s$ dollars.\n\nSo, the total cost can be written as\n\n$$\ns + 18\n$$\n\nSince the total ticket price is $\\$45$, we equate this expression to $45$.\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ns + 18 = 45\n$$\n\nTo isolate $s$, we need to perform the opposite of adding $18$, which is subtracting $18$.\n\nSo, we subtract $18$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns + 18 &= 45 \\\\\ns + 18 - 18 &= 45 - 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns + 0 &= 27 \\\\\ns &= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the service charge is $\\$27$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186232035 1064 "Many real-world situations involve an unknown quantity that we can determine using an equation. By translating a real-world description into an equation, we can solve for the unknown value.\n\nFor example, a concert ticket costs a base fee of $\\[math]18$ plus a service charge of $s$ dollars. If the total price of the ticket is $\\[/math]45$, what is the value of $s$?\n\nThe total cost is made up of two parts:\n\n- the base ticket fee of $\\[math]18$, and\n- the service charge of $s$ dollars.\n\nSo, the total cost can be written as\n\n$$\ns + 18\n$$\n\nSince the total ticket price is $\\[/math]45$, we equate this expression to $45$.\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ns + 18 = 45\n$$\n\nTo isolate $s$, we need to perform the opposite of adding $18$, which is subtracting $18$.\n\nSo, we subtract $18$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns + 18 &= 45 \\\\\ns + 18 - 18 &= 45 - 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns + 0 &= 27 \\\\\ns &= 27\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the service charge is $\\[math]27$." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186232039 1064 "Sometimes we need to find the total cost of a rectangular prism when we know the cost per cubic unit.\n\nTo solve these problems:\n\n1. Find the volume.\n2. Multiply the volume by the cost per cubic unit.\n\nBe sure the units match. Volume is measured in cubic units (such as $\\text{ft}^3$ or $\\text{in}^3$), and the rate is given \"per cubic unit.\"\n\nFor example, suppose a block of soap is shaped like a rectangular prism that is $2\\,\\textrm{in}$ long,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nwide, and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3}\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nhigh, and each cubic inch of soap costs $\\$4$.\n\n![](images/3b/3bef34cddbb058127f9af832bd6ca4e678ae61091820d3ca9e297fa48b3a9f59.png)\n\nTo find the cost of the soap block, we first find the volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4}{1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{24}{6} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the block has a volume of $4\\,\\text{in}^3$. Since each cubic inch costs $\\$4$, we get\n\n$$\n4 \\times \\$4 = \\$16\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total cost of the soap block is $\\$16$. Always find the volume first, then multiply by the unit cost." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186232039 1064 "Sometimes we need to find the total cost of a rectangular prism when we know the cost per cubic unit.\n\nTo solve these problems:\n\n1. Find the volume.\n2. Multiply the volume by the cost per cubic unit.\n\nBe sure the units match. Volume is measured in cubic units (such as $\\text{ft}^3$ or $\\text{in}^3$), and the rate is given \"per cubic unit.\"\n\nFor example, suppose a block of soap is shaped like a rectangular prism that is $2\\,\\textrm{in}$ long,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2}\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nwide, and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3}\\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nhigh, and each cubic inch of soap costs $\\[math]4$.\n\n![](images/3b/3bef34cddbb058127f9af832bd6ca4e678ae61091820d3ca9e297fa48b3a9f59.png)\n\nTo find the cost of the soap block, we first find the volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4}{1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{24}{6} \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the block has a volume of $4\\,\\text{in}^3$. Since each cubic inch costs $\\[/math]4$, we get\n\n$$\n4 \\times \\[math]4 = \\[/math]16\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total cost of the soap block is $\\[math]16$. Always find the volume first, then multiply by the unit cost." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186232040 1064 "Many real-world situations involve an unknown quantity that we want to determine. By translating the description of the situation into an equation, we can represent the relationship between the quantities.\n\nFor example, a concert ticket costs a base fee of $\\$15$ plus a service charge of $s$ dollars. If the total price of the ticket is $\\$42$, which equation can be used to find $s$?\n\nThe total cost is made up of two parts:\n\n- the base ticket fee of $\\$15$, and\n- the service charge of $s$ dollars.\n\nSo, the total cost can be written as\n\n$$\ns + 15\n$$\n\nSince the total ticket price is $\\$42$, we equate this expression to $42$.\n\nTherefore, the correct equation is\n\n$$\ns + 15 = 42\n$$\n\nLet's see more examples." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186232040 1064 "Many real-world situations involve an unknown quantity that we want to determine. By translating the description of the situation into an equation, we can represent the relationship between the quantities.\n\nFor example, a concert ticket costs a base fee of $\\[math]15$ plus a service charge of $s$ dollars. If the total price of the ticket is $\\[/math]42$, which equation can be used to find $s$?\n\nThe total cost is made up of two parts:\n\n- the base ticket fee of $\\[math]15$, and\n- the service charge of $s$ dollars.\n\nSo, the total cost can be written as\n\n$$\ns + 15\n$$\n\nSince the total ticket price is $\\[/math]42$, we equate this expression to $42$.\n\nTherefore, the correct equation is\n\n$$\ns + 15 = 42\n$$\n\nLet's see more examples." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186232048 1064 "Sometimes we need two conversions to change a measurement into a larger unit.\n\nFor example, to convert inches to yards, we can convert\n\n$$\n\\textrm{inches} \\rightarrow \\textrm{feet} \\rightarrow \\textrm{yards}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $360$ inches into yards. We first convert $360$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $360$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{ft} = 12\\,\\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\,\\textrm{ft} = 1\\,\\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $360$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $360{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 360 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 360 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{360}{12}ft|= 360in\n$$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{360}{12}$ as the division $360 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$0$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$0$$0$Therefore,$360\\,\\textrm{in} = 30\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $30$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd} = 1\\,\\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $30$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $30{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 30 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 30 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{30}{3}yd|= 30ft; 10yd|= 30ft\n$$Therefore,$30\\,\\textrm{ft} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion,\n\n$$\n360\\,\\textrm{in} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nTo convert inches to yards, we convert inches to feet first, then convert feet to yards." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186232048 1064 "Sometimes we need two conversions to change a measurement into a larger unit.\n\nFor example, to convert inches to yards, we can convert\n\n$$\n\\textrm{inches} \\rightarrow \\textrm{feet} \\rightarrow \\textrm{yards}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $360$ inches into yards. We first convert $360$ inches into feet, and then convert the result from feet to yards.\n\n- To convert from $360$ inches into feet, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{ft} = 12\\,\\textrm{in}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $12$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{12}\\,\\textrm{ft} = 1\\,\\textrm{in}$We want to determine how many feet are in $360$ inches. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $360{:}$$$\n\\displaystyle 360 \\cdot \\frac{1}{12}ft|= 360 \\cdot 1in; \\frac{360}{12}ft|= 360in\n$$Now, we simplify this fraction by interpreting $\\dfrac{360}{12}$ as the division $360 \\div 12$. We can calculate this using long division:$3$$0$$12$$\\!\\!\\enclose{longdiv}{3 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 6 {\\:\\phantom{\\mid}} 0}$$-\\!\\!\\!\\!$$3$$6$$0$$0$Therefore,$360\\,\\textrm{in} = 30\\,\\textrm{ft}$.\n- Next, to convert from $30$ feet into yards, we start with the unit conversion$1\\,\\textrm{yd} = 3\\,\\textrm{ft}$.Now, we divide both sides of the above equation by $3$ and obtain the following:$\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{yd} = 1\\,\\textrm{ft}$We want to determine how many yards are in $30$ feet. So, we multiply both sides of the above equation by $30{:}$$$\n\\displaystyle 30 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}yd|= 30 \\cdot 1ft; \\frac{30}{3}yd|= 30ft; 10yd|= 30ft\n$$Therefore,$30\\,\\textrm{ft} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}$.\n\nIn conclusion,\n\n$$\n360\\,\\textrm{in} = 10\\,\\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nTo convert inches to yards, we convert inches to feet first, then convert feet to yards." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186233428 1074 "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}\\approx 1+5x+10{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.1$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.1)^{5} & =(1+0.1)^{5} \\\\  & \\approx 1+5(0.1)+10(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =1+5(0.1)+10(0.01) \\\\  & =1+0.5+0.1 \\\\  & =1.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.1)^{5}-1.6|}{(1.1)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & \\approx 0.65%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186233428 1074 "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}\\approx 1+5x+10{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.1$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.1)^{5} & =(1+0.1)^{5} \\\\  & \\approx 1+5(0.1)+10(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =1+5(0.1)+10(0.01) \\\\  & =1+0.5+0.1 \\\\  & =1.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.1)^{5}-1.6|}{(1.1)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & \\approx 0.65\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186233467 1071 "![](images/e1/e1ada2941e9819e75218f9995d1913d535889c71b29efbf39ef73d91f9113205.png)\n\nThe function $h$ is a part of the graph of the function $y=\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of weeks $x$ at which the stock price given by this function was $$5,000.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $h$ is measured in thousands of dollars.*\n\n$x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186233467 1071 "![](images/e1/e1ada2941e9819e75218f9995d1913d535889c71b29efbf39ef73d91f9113205.png)\n\nThe function $h$ is a part of the graph of the function $y=\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3,$ as shown in the figure above. Find the number of weeks $x$ at which the stock price given by this function was $\\$5,000.$\n\n*Hint: Remember that $h$ is measured in thousands of dollars.*\n\n$x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186233555 1071 "If you deposit $$12,000$ into an account with a $8%$ annual interest rate, compounded quarterly, how many years will it take until there is at least $$18,000$ in the account?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186233555 1071 "If you deposit $\\$12,000$ into an account with a $8\\%$ annual interest rate, compounded quarterly, how many years will it take until there is at least $\\$18,000$ in the account?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186233555 1074 "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded quarterly, and there are $4$ quarters in one year, so $n=4.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=12,000$ and annual rate of $r=0.08.$\n\nTo find the number of years it will take until $P\\ge 18,000,$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ 12,000{(1+\\frac{0.08}{4})}^{4t} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ (1+0.02)^{4t} & \\ge \\frac{18,000}{12,000} \\\\ (1.02)^{4t} & \\ge 1.5 \\\\ log\\,({1.02}^{4t}) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4tlog\\,(1.02) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4t & \\ge \\frac{log\\,(1.5)}{log\\,(1.02)} \\\\ 4t & \\ge 20.475… \\\\ t & \\ge \\frac{20.475…}{4}\\approx 5.12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember, payments are compounded quarterly, so we must choose either $5$ or $5.25$ years.\n\n- After $5$ years, there is still less than $$18,000$ in the account, because $5<5.12.$\n\n- After $5.25$ years, there will be more than $$18,000$ in the account, since $5.25>5.12.$\n\nTherefore, it will take $5.25$ years ($5$ years and $1$ quarter) until there is at least $$18,000$ in the account." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186233555 1074 "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded quarterly, and there are $4$ quarters in one year, so $n=4.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=12,000$ and annual rate of $r=0.08.$\n\nTo find the number of years it will take until $P\\ge 18,000,$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ 12,000{(1+\\frac{0.08}{4})}^{4t} & \\ge 18,000 \\\\ (1+0.02)^{4t} & \\ge \\frac{18,000}{12,000} \\\\ (1.02)^{4t} & \\ge 1.5 \\\\ log\\,({1.02}^{4t}) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4tlog\\,(1.02) & \\ge log\\,(1.5) \\\\ 4t & \\ge \\frac{log\\,(1.5)}{log\\,(1.02)} \\\\ 4t & \\ge 20.475… \\\\ t & \\ge \\frac{20.475…}{4}\\approx 5.12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember, payments are compounded quarterly, so we must choose either $5$ or $5.25$ years.\n\n- After $5$ years, there is still less than $\\$18,000$ in the account, because $5<5.12.$\n\n- After $5.25$ years, there will be more than $\\$18,000$ in the account, since $5.25>5.12.$\n\nTherefore, it will take $5.25$ years ($5$ years and $1$ quarter) until there is at least $\\$18,000$ in the account." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186233559 1071 "If the company sells ten couches in a week, the weekly profit will be $$4\\,800.$ Find an expression for $b$ in terms of $a.$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186233559 1071 "If the company sells ten co