produce$\\,$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ couches per week." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186233563 1074 "From part 5, the required number of sofas $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n12\\cdot ({x}^{2}-50x+600)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $12,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-50x+600=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(600)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-50).$ The required factors are $(-20)$ and $(-30).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-50x=-20x-30x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-50x+600 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-20x-30x+600 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-20x-30+600 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-20x)+(-30x+600) & =0 \\\\ x(x-20)-30(x-20) & =0 \\\\ (x-30)(x-20) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=20$ or $x=30.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the company needs to produce $20$ couches per week to make a profit of $$7\\,200.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186233563 1074 "From part 5, the required number of sofas $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n12\\cdot ({x}^{2}-50x+600)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $12,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-50x+600=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(600)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-50).$ The required factors are $(-20)$ and $(-30).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-50x=-20x-30x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-50x+600 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-20x-30x+600 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-20x-30+600 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-20x)+(-30x+600) & =0 \\\\ x(x-20)-30(x-20) & =0 \\\\ (x-30)(x-20) & 