\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded monthly, and there are $12$ months in one year, so $n=12.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=5,000,$ an annual interest of $r=0.01,$ and a length of $t=15$ years. Substituting these values into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} \\\\  & =5,000{(1+\\frac{0.01}{12})}^{12(15)} \\\\  & =5,000(1+0.000\\,833…)^{180} \\\\  & =5,000(1.000\\,833…)^{180} \\\\  & =5,000(1.161\\,76…) \\\\  & \\approx 5,809.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, after $15$ years, Mike will have $$5,809$ in the account." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186233688 1074 "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded monthly, and there are $12$ months in one year, so $n=12.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=5,000,$ an annual interest of $r=0.01,$ and a length of $t=15$ years. Substituting these values into the formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} \\\\  & =5,000{(1+\\frac{0.01}{12})}^{12(15)} \\\\  & =5,000(1+0.000\\,833…)^{180} \\\\  & =5,000(1.000\\,833…)^{180} \\\\  & =5,000(1.161\\,76…) \\\\  & \\approx 5,809.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, after $15$ years, Mike will have $\\$5,809$ in the account." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186233689 1071 "A principal of $$102,000$ is invested in the stock market and earns interest at a rate of $2%$ per year, compounded daily. To the nearest dollar, how much will the investment with worth after $7$ years (ignore the leap years)?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186233689 1071 "A principal of $\\$102,000$ is invested in the stock market and earns interest at a rate of $2\\%$ per year, compounded daily. To the nearest dollar, how much will the investment with worth after $7$ years (ignore the leap years)?" 131941395394