he account $6$ years ago (ignore the leap years)? Round your answer to the nearest dollar." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186233690 1074 "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded daily, and there are $365$ days in one year, so $n=365.$ We also have an annual rate of $r=0.03,$ and a final amount of $P=37,500$ after $t=6$ years. To find the initial deposit, ${P}_{0},$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for ${P}_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} \\\\ 37,500 & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{0.03}{365})}^{365(6)} \\\\ 37,500 & ={P}_{0}(1.000\\,082…)^{2,190} \\\\ 37,500 & ={P}_{0}(1.197…) \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{37,500}{1.197…} \\\\ {P}_{0} & \\approx 31,323\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $6$ years ago, the savings account had an initial value of $$31,323.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186233690 1074 "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded daily, and there are $365$ days in one year, so $n=365.$ We also have an annual rate of $r=0.03,$ and a final amount of $P=37,500$ after $t=6$ years. To find the initial deposit, ${P}_{0},$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for ${P}_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} \\\\ 37,500 & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{0.03}{365})}^{365(6)} \\\\ 37,500 & ={P}_{0}(1.000\\,082…)^{2,190} \\\\ 37,500 & ={P}_{0}(1.197…) \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{37,500}{1.197…} \\\\ {P}_{0} & \\approx 31,323\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $6$ years ago, the savings account had an initial value of $\\$31,323.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186233705 1074 "The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,20),$ which means that the cost is $$20$ even when no time has been elapsed. So, the boat rental charges an initial fee of $$20.00.$\n\nTo find the cost per hour, notice that the units of the slope are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{dollars}}{\\text{hours}},\n$$\n\nor dollars per hour. So, to calculate the cost per hour, we need to calculate the slope of the line. \n\nSince the line passes through the points $(0,20)$ and $(4,40)$, we find its slope as follows:\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-20}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{4} \\\\  & =5\\text{ dollars/hour}\\end{aligned}\n\n$$$\n\nSo, the cost of a ride is $$20.00$ plus $$5.00$ per hour." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186233705 1074 "The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,20),$ which means that the cost is $\\$20$ even when no time has been elapsed. So, the boat rental charges an initial fee of $\\$20.00.$\n\nTo find the cost per hour, notice that the units of the slope are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{dollars}}{\\text{hours}},\n$$\n\nor dollars per hour. So, to calculate the cost per hour, we need to calculate the slope of the line. \n\nSince the line passes through the points $(0,20)$ and $(4,40)$, we find its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-20}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{4} \\\\  & =5\\text{ dollars/hour}\\end{aligned}\n\n$$\n\nSo, the cost of a ride is $\\$20.00$ plus $\\$5.00$ per hour." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186233773 1074 "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\sin{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi \\left(x^{2} +2x - 2\\right).\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\cos{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\pi (2x + 2).\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\cos u \\cdot \\pi (2x+2)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (2x+2)\\cos u\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (2x+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(x^{2} +2x - 2\\right) \\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(1) & =\\pi (2\\cdot1+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(1^{2} +2\\cdot1 - 2\\right) \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\n& =4\\pi \\cos(\\pi) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 4\\pi \\cdot (-1) \\\\[3pt]\n&= -4\\pi\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is \n\\[\nm = f'(1) = \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-4\\pi}.\n\\]" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186233773 1074 "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = \\sin{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi \\left(x^{2} +2x - 2\\right).\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\cos{u}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\pi (2x + 2).\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\cos u \\cdot \\pi (2x+2)\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (2x+2)\\cos u\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\pi (2x+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(x^{2} +2x - 2\\right) \\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n\\begin{align}\nf'(1) & =\\pi (2\\cdot1+2)\\cos \\left(  \\pi \\left(1^{2} +2\\cdot1 - 2\\right) \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\n& =4\\pi \\cos(\\pi) \\\\[3pt]\n&= 4\\pi \\cdot (-1) \\\\[3pt]\n&= -4\\pi\n\\end{align}\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is \n\\[\nm = f'(1) = \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-4\\pi}.\n\\]" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186233784 1074 "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\ny = 8\\csc{u}, \\qquad u = 10x\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -8\\csc u \\cot u \\qquad \\style{font-family:inherit;}{\\text{and}} \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 10.\n\\]\n\