e $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{5}\\csc^2\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right).\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186233786 1074 "The slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n\\[\nf = \\cot{u}, \\qquad u = 1-\\dfrac x 5.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = -\\csc^2 u \\qquad \\text{and}\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\dfrac{1}{5}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=-\\csc^2 u \\cdot  \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac{1}{5}\\csc^2 \\left(1-\\dfrac{x}{5}\\right).\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\n\\begin{eqnarray}\n    \\eqalign{\n\nf'(x) & =\\dfrac 1 5\\csc^2\\left(1-\\dfrac 1 5 \\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\t& =\\dfrac 1 5\\csc^2\\left(\\dfrac 4 5 \\right) .\n       \n    }\n\\end{eqnarray}\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{5}\\csc^2\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right).\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186233932 1074 "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ is given by $f'\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. We express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\\[\nf = \\sec{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi x^{2}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec{u}\\tan{u}, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\pi x.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} & = \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\sec{u}\\tan{u} \\cdot 2\\pi x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= 2\\pi x \\sec{\\left(\\pi x^{2}\\right)}\\tan{\\left(\\pi x^{2}\\right)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we find \n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) &= 2\\pi \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sec{\\left[\\pi\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\\right]}\\tan{\\left[\\pi\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\\right]} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\pi \\sec{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)} \\tan{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\pi \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\pi\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\pi\\sqrt{2}}.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186233932 1074 "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ is given by $f'\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. We express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\\[\nf = \\sec{u}, \\qquad u = \\pi x^{2}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec{u}\\tan{u}, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\pi x.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} & = \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\sec{u}\\tan{u} \\cdot 2\\pi x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= 2\\pi x \\sec{\\left(\\pi x^{2}\\right)}\\tan{\\left(\\pi x^{2}\\right)}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we find \n\\begin{eqnarray}\n \\eqalign{\nf'\\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) &= 2\\pi \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot \\sec{\\left[\\pi\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\\right]}\\tan{\\left[\\pi\\cdot\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}\\right]} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\pi \\sec{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)} \\tan{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\pi \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\pi\\sqrt{2}.}\n\\end{eqnarray}\n\nTherefore, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\pi\\sqrt{2}}.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186233933 1074 "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\\[\nf = \\csc{u}, \\qquad u = \\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = -\\csc{u}\\cot{u}  \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} x^{2}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\left(-\\csc{u}\\cot{u}\\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} x^{2} \\\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} x^{2} \\csc{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}\\right)\\cot{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}\\right)}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f'(1)& =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\cdot 1^2 \\cdot \\csc{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\cot{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)}} \\\\[5pt] \n\n&= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\[5pt]\n\n& = -\\pi\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,2\\right) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-y_1 &= m(x-x_1)\\\\[5pt]\n\ny-2 &= -\\pi\\sqrt{3}(x-1)\\\\[5pt]\n\ny &= \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-\\pi\\sqrt{3}}x + \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\pi\\sqrt{3} + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186233933 1074 "First, the slope of $y = f(x)$ at $x = 1$ is given by $f'(1).$\n\nWe will calculate $f'(x)$ using the chain rule. First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\\[\nf = \\csc{u}, \\qquad u = \\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = -\\csc{u}\\cot{u}  \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} x^{2}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\\begin{align*}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x} &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\left(-\\csc{u}\\cot{u}\\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} x^{2} \\\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} x^{2} \\csc{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}\\right)\\cot{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi x^{3}}{6}\\right)}}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we find\n\\begin{align}\nm=f'(1)& =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\cdot 1^2 \\cdot \\csc{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)\\cot{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)}} \\\\[5pt] \n\n&= -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\[5pt]\n\n& = -\\pi\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{align}\n\nFinally, we find the equation of the tangent at the point $\\left(1,2\\right) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n\\begin{align*}\ny-y_1 &= m(x-x_1)\\\\[5pt]\n\ny-2 &= -\\pi\\sqrt{3}(x-1)\\\\[5pt]\n\ny &= \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-\\pi\\sqrt{3}}x + \\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\pi\\sqrt{3} + 2}\n\\end{align*}" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186233934 1074 "First, we express\n\\[\nf(x) = -2(\\sin{(x)})^{3}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\\[\nf = -2u^{3}, \\qquad u = \\sin{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm df}{\\textrm d u} = -6u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf'(x) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x}\\\\[5pt]\n\n& = -6u^2 \\cdot \\cos{(x)}\\\\[5pt]\n\n&=\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{-6\\sin^2{(x)}\\cos{(x)}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186233934 1074 "First, we express\n\\[\nf(x) = -2(\\sin{(x)})^{3}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\\[\nf = -2u^{3}, \\qquad u = \\sin{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\textrm df}{\\textrm d u} = -6u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{(x)}.\n\\]\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, 