aligned}P={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} & \\ge 4,000 \\\\ 2,000{(1+\\frac{0.2}{2})}^{2t} & \\ge 4,000 \\\\ (1+0.1)^{2t} & \\ge \\frac{4,000}{2,000} \\\\ (1.1)^{2t} & \\ge 2 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({1.1}^{2t}) & \\ge \\operatorname{log}(2) \\\\ 2t\\operatorname{log}(1.1) & \\ge \\operatorname{log}(2) \\\\ 2t & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{log}(2)}{\\operatorname{log}(1.1)} \\\\ 2t & \\ge 7.272… \\\\ t & \\ge \\frac{7.272…}{2}\\approx 3.64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember payments are compounded semi-annually, so we must choose either $3.5$ or $4$ years.\n\n- After $3.5$ years, there is still less than $$4,000$ in the account, because $3.5<3.64.$\n\n- After $4$ years, there will be more than $$4,000$ in the account, since $4>3.64.$\n\nTherefore, it will take $4$ years until there is at least $$4,000$ in the account." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186234266 1074 "The compound interest equation states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & ={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe annual interest is compounded semi-annually, so $n=2.$ We also have an initial amount of ${P}_{0}=2,000$ and annual rate of $r=0.2.$\n\nTo find the number of years it will take until $P\\ge 4,000,$ we substitute these values into the formula and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={P}_{0}{(1+\\frac{r}{n})}^{nt} & \\ge 4,000 \\\\ 2,000{(1+\\frac{0.2}{2})}^{2t} & \\ge 4,000 \\\\ (1+0.1)^{2t} & \\ge \\frac{4,000}{2,000} \\\\ (1.1)^{2t} & \\ge 2 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}({1.1}^{2t}) & \\ge \\operatorname{log}(2) \\\\ 2t\\operatorname{log}(1.1) & \\ge \\operatorname{log}(2) \\\\ 2t & \\ge \\frac{\\operatorname{log}(2)}{\\operatorname{log}(1.1)} \\\\ 2t & \\ge 7.272… \\\\ t & \\ge \\frac{7.272…}{2}\\approx 3.64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember payments are compounded semi-annually, so we must choose either $3.5$ or $4$ years.\n\n- After $3.5$ years, there is still less than $\\$4,000$ in the account, because $3.5<3.64.$\n\n- After $4$ years, there will be more than $\\$4,000$ in t